neural networks.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.710
And today, I want to build with
you another kind of network,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.660
a recurrent neural network.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.520
So let's go.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.590
A couple of reminders
from yesterday.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.540
You remember, we started with
this initial one layer neural

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.560
network.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.460
And I need you to
remember the formula

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.380
for one layer of
a neural network

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.670
because we will be using it.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.500
So we were reading in pixels.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.770
But it works for any
input vector, of course.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.090
And you remember, we
said that the neurons

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.500
do weighted sums of all of
their inputs, they add in bias,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.050
and they feed that through
some activation function.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.330
Here, softmax.

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.590
It can be another function.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.320
It's just a function,
value in, value out.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.130
But usually, in neural networks,
it's a non-linear function.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:28.410
And we wrote this one
layer neural network

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.780
using a matrix multiply,
blah, blah, blah,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.470
we've seen all that,
as this formula.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:40.290
And you remember, we did
this not for just one image,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.440
but we actually
wrote this formula

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.120
processing 100 images at a time.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.720
So in x, we have
a batch of images,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.880
a whole batch, 100 images.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.270
And then x times w are all the
weighted sums for our neurons.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.320
We add the biases.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.430
We feed that through
our activation function.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.340
And we obtain a
batch of predictions.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.140
So in our case, since we were
classifying handwritten digits,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.699
those predictions
are 10 numbers,

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:13.750
which are the probabilities of
these digits being a 0, a 1, 2,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.540
3, and so on.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.430
And so we obtained
those probabilities

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.790
as the outputs of
our 10 neurons.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.280
OK, so whenever you
see this formula,

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.650
we will see it
again a lot today,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.650
you think, one layer
of a neural network.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.020
OK?

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:34.560
And then also, what I
need you to remember

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:41.130
is that once we get our output
from the neural network,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:45.540
the way we train it is
that we give it examples.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.870
It produces some prediction.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.540
And then we say, no, no, no, no,
no, that's not what we wanted.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.270
This is what you should predict.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.130
We give it the correct answer.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.280
And to do that, we have to
encode this correct answer

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.900
in a similar format.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.710
So it's called-- it's a
very basic type of encoding.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.750
It's called one hot encoding.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.260
And basically here, if
we have 10 categories,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:16.210
specifying one answer category
means encoding it as 10 0s,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.970
but just one 1 somewhere in the
middle, and the index of the 1

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.920
here, it's at index 6.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.460
Means that the correct
answer was a 6, OK?

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.520
So in this shape, it
becomes possible to compute

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.320
a distance between what
the network predicts

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.240
and what we know to be true.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.450
And that distance, we call
that our error function.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.700
Or sometimes, it's
called the loss function.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.420
That's what we use to
guide our training.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:50.160
So during training, we give it
an example, produces an output.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.740
We say, no, no, no,
that's not what we wanted.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.790
Compute the distance between
what the network says

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.440
and what we know to be true.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.160
And from that distance,
we derive the gradient.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.690
And then, we follow
the gradient.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.670
And that modifies the
weights and biases.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.240
And that's what
training is about, OK?

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:15.700
So now, let's look at
this neural network.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.890
So it should look familiar.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.380
It's a vector as an input.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.790
It has a middle layer using
the hyperbolic tangent

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.020
as an activation function.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.170
So we've seen the
sigmoid last time,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.890
which is let's say the simplest
possible function going

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.700
from 0 to 1 continuously.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.500
The hyperbolic tangent is the
simplest possible function

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:40.170
going from minus 1
to 1 continuously.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.200
It's just a sigmoid shifted.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.510
And then, a second
layer, which is a softmax

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.730
layer so that we
read something out.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:53.970
But the specificity is
here that the output

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:58.590
of this intermediate
green layer is actually

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:04.100
fed back in the next
time step in the inputs.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:09.260
So the real input into one cell
of a recurrent neural network

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:15.230
is the input concatenated to
the output of the inner layer

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:16.500
from the previous step.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.350
And we call this the state.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.610
So it's actually
a state machine.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:22.940
You feed it inputs.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.170
It produces outputs.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.560
But you also feed it a state.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.140
It produces an output state,
which you feed back in,

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.270
in the next time step.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.780
And that's why it's called
a recurrent neural network,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.230
it's because it is
applied on time sequences.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:43.350
At each step in time, you
feed in one input vector,

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:47.810
concatenate it to
the previous state.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.580
Turn the crank once.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:54.140
That produces some outputs from
this middle layer, as well as

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.490
a result. And you feed
that back as the new input

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:02.810
state for the next
x input, which

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.070
you have in your sequence.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.820
So it can be represented.

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.130
I'm showing you
the neurons inside.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:15.050
But here, it's basically the API
of one recurrent neural network

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:15.770
cell.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:16.640
It has an input.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.530
It has an output, which
you then usually feed

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:24.260
into a softmax layer
to make sense of it,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:25.640
to produce predictions.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.440
I mean, probabilities.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:30.440
And it has an input state
that produces an output

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.700
state that you loop back
in as the input state.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.820
That's the state machine part.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.770
OK?

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:43.250
So now, well yes, and
the parameter for this

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.640
is the internal size
of this middle layer.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.350
That's what is adjustable.

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.450
Usually, your input is
whatever your input is.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.480
And your output is whatever
you're trying to predict.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.630
So those are not
adjustable parameters.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.060
So here it is
written in equations.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.420
Again, the input
is the real input

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.760
at time t concatenated
to the previous state.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.860
Then, we feed that through.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.370
Here, you should recognize
one layer of a neural network.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.770
You should recognize
this formula

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.990
using the hyperbolic tangent
as an activation function.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:22.040
So I put it over there.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.120
And this produces
an output, Ht, which

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:32.990
is both used as our new
state and as the output that

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:35.330
will be fed into
the softmax layer

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:41.390
to actually produce a vector of
probabilities between 0 and 1.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:43.430
OK?

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.610
So now, how do we
train this thing?

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:52.610
So typically, this is used for
natural language processing,

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:53.690
for instance.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:57.290
So a typical input
will be a character.

00:07:57.290 --> 00:08:03.020
And a character will be, again,
one-hot encoded into let's say

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.800
100 competent vectors
if we are using--

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.050
we will be using
here an alphabet

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.870
of 100 possible characters.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:18.980
So one character is encoded into
a 100 element vector, so 99 0s

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.710
and a 1 at the ASCII
index of that character.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:28.540
So we put a character in.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.520
We propagate through
the neural networks.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.820
We propagate through
the softmax layer.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.730
We obtain a character
as an output.

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:43.510
If that is not the
character we wanted, well,

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.580
we compute the difference
between what he said

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.450
and what we know to be true and
use retro propagation to fix

00:08:49.450 --> 00:08:53.800
the weights and biases inside of
the cell to get better results.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:56.620
That is very classical training.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:01.030
But what if the
result was wrong not

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.990
because the weights and biases
inside of the cell was wrong,

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:10.920
but because the input, the state
input, H minus 1, was wrong?

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.230
That input is a constant
in this problem.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:21.290
There's not much
you can do about it.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.410
So here, we are stuck.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.670
What is the solution?

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.620
Well, the solution is
to replicate the cell.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.590
And now, so this is a replica.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:36.550
It's reusing the exact
same weights, OK?

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:41.200
Now, let's see the
output, Y1, is bad.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.520
I say, no, that's not it.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.590
This was the correct
output I'm training.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.740
So I know what the correct
output is supposed to be.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.250
So from that, I compute
the enter, the gradient.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.430
I retro propagate.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.120
I can fix the weights
and biases in the cells

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.840
to get a better output.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:00.580
And if needed, I can fix
the weights and biases

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:05.860
to get a better H0, the state
flowing between those two

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.760
stages of the cell.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.740
So now, I have a
handle on at least H0.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:14.410
I still have no handle
at all on H minus 1.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.120
If it is H minus
1 that was wrong,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:19.330
there is nothing I can do.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:23.110
So that is how you train
recurrent neural networks.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:27.640
You have to unroll them
across a certain length

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:32.470
and give them a sequence
of, let's say, characters.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.960
It will produce a sequence
of output characters.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:36.550
If you are training,
you know what

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.170
the answer was supposed to be.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.560
So you use that to compute
your error function,

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:45.820
do you retro propagation,
adjust the weights and biases.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:50.280
And it will work to
a certain extent.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.020
To what extent?

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.110
Oh, yes, small detail,
if you want to go deep,

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.540
you can actually
stack the cells.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:10:59.290
Why?

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:06.320
Well, two cells stacked like
this, the API remains the same.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.690
It's still an input.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.650
It's still an output that
feeds into a softmax layer.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.080
And there is still an input
state and an output state

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:14.110
that you feedback in.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.750
It's just that the output
state now is slightly bigger.

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.480
So that's how you go deep in
the recurrent neural network.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.370
You stack those cells,
and that becomes

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.370
a new cell which still
has input, output,

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:27.100
input state, output state.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:28.600
And of course, you unroll it.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.410
So let's take this sentence.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.700
Let's say now, we use
not characters but words

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:35.020
as our inputs.

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:38.830
Of course, there are
technical problems doing that.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:42.140
A typical alphabet is
maybe 100 characters.

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:46.430
A typical vocabulary
is around 30,000 words.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.180
So here, one-hot
encoding gives you

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:53.770
a vector of 30,000
components for each word.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:55.450
It's a bit heavy.

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:57.760
I won't go into the details
of how you handle that.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:02.300
It's called embedding.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.130
Whatever, let's just assume
that we solved this problem.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:07.690
So we have this sentence.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:10.810
Michael was born in Paris,
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.900
And at the end, we have
his mother tongue is.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.610
So if we train this
model on English,

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:20.860
probably it will
have figured out

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.110
that "his mother
tongue is" is followed

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:25.630
by the name of a
language, like English,

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:28.470
German, or Russian, something.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.210
Here, however,
the correct answer

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:36.250
is French because this
guy was born in France.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:39.070
So let's imagine
that we feed in,

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:41.860
we have unrolled this
neural network over,

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.320
let's say, 30 words.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.020
Or let's say 10
words here, 10 words.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:49.340
And at the end, we have
his mother tongue is,

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:53.470
and we are asking the network to
predict what is the next word.

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:58.000
And the network says, English.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.930
So now, what we want to
do is put on the outputs

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:03.760
a sentence that says,
blah, blah, blah,

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:05.890
his mother tongue is French.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:09.070
And do retro propagation.

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:13.450
But for this to
work, the beginning

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:15.760
of the sentence, the part
where the information

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:18.400
about Paris and
where he's born is,

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.200
has to be part of that example.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.070
And that example is
longer than 10 words,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:26.770
which is our unroll size.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:32.460
There is simply
no way whatsoever

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:37.060
of putting that correct
example plus correct output

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:40.510
into a network that we
unrolled over only 10 words

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.090
because the distance
is more than 10.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:45.350
And that's a
fundamental limitation.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:50.440
If you want to capture this
information, this behavior

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:54.130
that, if he was born in France,
probably his mother language

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:57.010
is French, you
will have to unroll

00:13:57.010 --> 00:14:01.420
this network over a
long enough sequence

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:05.710
to be able to input this
full example into it.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:08.990
And if you do that, you will
probably unroll it here over,

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:10.630
how many, 50 words?

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:12.730
Something like that.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.120
If you do that, the
problem is that you end up

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:19.480
with a very deep neural network.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.880
Yesterday, we've seen neural
networks of five layers.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.290
The big ones, like
Inception and so on,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:29.260
are 40, 50, 60, 70 layers.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.660
You see here, we
have a toy example.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.100
And we already
see that we should

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:38.300
be going to 50 or 100
layers just to solve this.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:42.580
So in recurrent neural
networks, you always

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:47.260
end up using very
deep neural networks.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:51.430
And when I say deep, it's
because the state signal has

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.090
to go through all those cells.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.860
And remember, in each
cell, the state signal

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.580
is concatenated
to the input which

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:59.860
goes through a
neural network layer,

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.680
produces a new state, which
goes to the next cell, that

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:04.270
is concatenated to the input.

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.710
Goes to another
neural network layer.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:11.080
So from here to the end,
we traverse at least one

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.240
neural network layer per cell.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:16.990
That's how wide and deep.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:20.770
Deep neural networks
have a technical problem.

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:25.422
They tend not to converge
when you train them.

00:15:25.422 --> 00:15:27.130
I won't go into the
mathematical details.

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:29.350
It's called the vanishing
gradient problem.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:32.560
Basically, your
gradient becomes 0.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.560
And since you use your
gradient to go forward,

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.450
that's a bit of a problem.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:41.620
So a solution was invented.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:44.470
I won't go into the
mathematical explanations

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.330
of why this solution works.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:50.210
I just want you to
understand how it works.

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:56.410
So would you [INAUDIBLE] an
explanation using the arrow

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:59.650
soup of a diagram on the left?

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:04.750
Or the incomprehensible
equations on the right?

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:07.138
Which one do you prefer?

00:16:07.138 --> 00:16:08.110
AUDIENCE: Arrows.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.026
MARTIN GORNER: Arrows?

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:12.460
I'm a developer.

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:15.740
And those equations look
a little bit like code.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.090
And I do code.

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:20.980
Sorry.

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.160
But on the arrows, you
see at least one thing.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:28.970
So I'll do some hand-waving
mathematics again.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:31.570
You see that the state is
actually split into two.

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:34.090
You have the H state
and the C state.

00:16:34.090 --> 00:16:39.490
And the C line there is actually
configured in such a way

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:42.460
that the network can decide
to persist information

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:46.900
on it, to leave it unchanged
from iteration to iteration.

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:50.230
And that is somehow
what explains

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:52.480
that even if you line
up many of those,

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:55.480
since it has the possibility of
leaving some part of the state

00:16:55.480 --> 00:17:00.700
unchanged, it goes around those
vanishing gradient problems.

00:17:00.700 --> 00:17:04.660
End of hand-waving mathematics.

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:05.470
So let's do it.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.349
Let's see how it
works in practice.

00:17:08.349 --> 00:17:12.609
And actually, it's based
on a concept of gates.

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:17.060
So again, we concatenate
the real input to the state

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.500
from the previous step.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:25.470
And we compute three, you
recognize the formulas,

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.880
neural network layers.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:31.940
The sigma is for the
sigmoid activation function.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:35.080
So the sigma outputs
values between 0 and 1.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.420
And we call those gates
because we will actually

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:42.862
be multiplying these numbers
to another vector to gate it.

00:17:42.862 --> 00:17:44.320
You know, if you
multiply something

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:47.830
by a very small value, there's
not much that goes through.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:50.800
If you multiply something by
something that is close to 1,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:52.900
almost all of the
information goes through.

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.490
So that's how we
will be using them.

00:17:55.490 --> 00:18:06.640
Now, our input becomes, well,
we have to size adapt our input.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.210
I put on the side the
sizes of all the vectors

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:11.410
we are working with.

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:14.590
That's just to tell you that
there is nothing to see there.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.550
Inside of the cell,
everything is of size n.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:21.280
That's the parameter
that you decide

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:23.890
as the size of your cell, OK?

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:27.410
But our inputs, they
are what they are.

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:30.730
So we first need one
neural network layer

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:34.180
to adapt the size of
our inputs to size n.

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:36.000
So that becomes our new input.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:42.730
And now, the C line,
the way you read this,

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.230
this is a kind of memory.

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:46.930
So the new state
of the memory is

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.320
the old state of the
memory without what

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.030
we chose to forget.

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:53.650
We multiply by this forget gate.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:57.220
This is a series of
numbers between 0 and 1.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:03.940
Plus what we chose to
remember from our new input.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.860
That's the way to read it.

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:10.340
So we multiply our new
input by the update gate.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.940
Again, numbers
between 0 and 1 that

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:15.220
shows which part
of the information

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:20.560
we want to retain from this
input into our internal memory.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:28.780
And then, our new state
is simply the memory--

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:30.790
the hyperbolic
tangent here, that's

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:32.530
not a neural network layer.

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:37.220
That's just a size adaptation
to put it between minus 1 and 1.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.250
So it's basically
the memory cell

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.740
multiplied by the result gate.

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:46.150
So here, we choose what
part of our internal memory

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:49.670
we want to expose to
the outside as a result.

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:51.910
So that's the physical
interpretation

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:53.050
of these equations.

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.030
We have those three gates.

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:57.670
We size adapt our input.

00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:00.490
And then, the new memory
is the old memory minus

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:02.320
what we want to forget
plus what we want

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:04.570
to remember from the input.

00:20:04.570 --> 00:20:09.220
And the result is this
memory cell modulo

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:13.120
what we want to actually expose
as an output at that step.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:13.840
OK?

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:18.280
And now, this HT will actually
become part of the new state,

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:21.790
and also drive the
softmax layer if we

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:24.490
add a softmax layer,
which is represented here

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:26.410
by this yellow circle.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:31.990
We usually represent the softmax
layer as external to a cell.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:34.630
So this is called an LSTM.

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:36.910
And this was
invented specifically

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:39.490
to make recurrent
neural networks work

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:42.520
and to solve this depth
problem that, if you

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:45.100
are unrolling over
a large sequence,

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.330
they tended not to converge.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.870
You will have to believe me
on the mathematics with this.

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:51.970
They converge.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.620
But you will have to--

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:59.260
I'm sure someone noticed
that this choice of equations

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:03.300
and this choice of arrows
was somehow arbitrary.

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:06.190
I mean, why point them
here and not there?

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:09.340
Many combinations exist.

00:21:09.340 --> 00:21:13.090
Lots of different variations
of those R and N cells

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:14.620
have been devised.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:17.470
And someone published
a paper, a recap paper,

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.910
where he tested all of
them and found them to do

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:21.130
all exactly the same thing.

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:29.050
So in the end, the one
we use is called the GRU.

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:30.550
And I won't go into the details.

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.610
It's basically a cheaper LSTM.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:34.870
Here are the equations.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:35.930
Not very different.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.180
Same API.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:41.980
But only two gates
instead of three gates.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.320
And each gate has
weights and biases.

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:47.680
So we save a part of
our computational cycle

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:51.580
not computing those
third weights and biases.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.260
OK, so we will use the GRU.

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:56.470
And now, let's implement
a neural network

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:59.980
that does a language model.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:05.470
So we will be training on
sequences of characters.

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:08.830
And when I say language
model, it's actually a network

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:11.650
that we will use to predict--

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.530
we will train it to predict
what the next character is.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:19.240
Like here, St. Joh,
I will teach it

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:22.960
to produce the same
sequence shifted by one.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.990
So actually, I will
teach it to understand

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:28.930
that the next character
should be an "n" because this

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:31.240
is St. John.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:32.230
So how do we do that?

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:40.450
In TensorFlow, now I'm using a
higher level API of TensorFlow

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:43.600
than what I had been
using yesterday.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:45.760
I just call GRUCell.

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:47.800
That creates a GRU cell.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:51.010
And I call this higher
level, because you've

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:54.010
seen this GRU cell has actually
a couple of neural network

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:55.420
layers inside.

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:56.290
It has two gates.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:57.790
That's at least two layers.

00:22:57.790 --> 00:23:00.850
So it has a host of
weights and biases

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:03.460
which are actually defined
in the background when

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:04.162
I call this.

00:23:04.162 --> 00:23:05.620
That's why it's a
higher level API.

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:10.990
It does its own weights and bias
declarations in the background.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:12.500
Now, I said we want to go deep.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.710
So let's stack this
cell three high.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:18.130
That's how we do deep
recurrent neural networks.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:19.780
Three is a TensorFlow
call for that.

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:21.370
It's called MultiRNNCell.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:22.990
Give it a cell.

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.390
You say how many times
you want to stack it.

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:28.150
And that gives you another cell.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.790
Because we have seen already
that these three stacked

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:34.170
cells actually have the
same API as one cell.

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:37.070
So you can use it as a new cell.

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:39.570
And now, we need to unroll this.

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.725
For that, we call
in TensorFlow flow

00:23:41.725 --> 00:23:46.030
this dynamic RNN function,
which is a bit of magic.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:49.460
And that's what will
unroll this sequence.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:51.370
So how many times?

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:53.290
You don't see it
in the parameters

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:56.710
because it's actually specified
in the shape of the input

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:58.790
tensor, x.

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:02.140
If this input tensor
has eight or let's

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.090
say 30 characters
in it, it will be

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:08.680
unrolled over a sequence
of 30 characters.

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:11.340
And actually, the
little part of magic,

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:14.710
really it's magic, we
will not be using it here,

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:20.260
but this dynamic RNN,
what it can do also,

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:23.840
remember that we will be
training this on batches,

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:24.340
as always.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:26.320
We always train on batches.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.840
So in this case,
all my batches will

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.354
be sequences of the same size.

00:24:31.354 --> 00:24:32.520
That's the case in my model.

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.550
In other models,
I might not have

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:39.730
sequences of the same size.

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:41.950
Dynamic RNN can handle that.

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.080
If you pass it a
batch of sequences,

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:46.330
even if they are not
of the same size,

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:49.750
alongside that you
pass the actual sizes,

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:53.620
and it will, for each
sentence in the batch,

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:57.000
unroll your network the
correct number of times.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:01.540
And then, also pass the
output from the correct stage.

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:02.830
It's super helpful.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.329
Will not be using it here
because all of our sequences

00:25:06.329 --> 00:25:07.120
have the same size.

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.970
But that is super helpful.

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:18.580
All right, so now, we need to
implement our softmax layer

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:25.470
from those H double
second 0 to H second 8.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:27.220
Well, basically the
outputs at the bottom.

00:25:31.300 --> 00:25:34.510
We know how to do a
softmax layer, OK?

00:25:34.510 --> 00:25:36.350
But here, since
we have unrolled,

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:40.300
remember each stack here is
a copy of the previous one.

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:41.800
We are sharing the weights.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:46.280
So on the softmax side, we have
to share the weights, as well.

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:49.180
So we could do this using
the TensorFlow APIs.

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:52.900
You know, define
one softmax layer.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:55.030
And then, for the
next one, call an API

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:57.160
that retrieves the weights
of the previous one

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:59.540
and reuses them.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:01.360
That's too complicated here.

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:05.240
Actually, there is a little
hack that you can use.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:09.670
Remember, we are always
training on batches, OK?

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:12.870
So this will be taking
a batch of sequences,

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:16.000
outputting a batch of sequences.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:18.790
Each sequence is a
sequence of characters.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:21.940
So what is the difference
between having,

00:26:21.940 --> 00:26:29.450
let's say, 8 softmax cells that
each process a batch of 100

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:34.550
characters, or having just
one that process 800 of them?

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.260
That's the same thing.

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:37.570
Let's just do one.

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:40.900
And we will put all of those
outputs in the same bag

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:42.515
and just use that one cell.

00:26:42.515 --> 00:26:44.640
Anyway, we were supposed
to be sharing the weights,

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:48.310
so defining just one cell is
a very good way of doing that.

00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:52.390
So that's what I do with
my reshape operation there.

00:26:52.390 --> 00:26:55.180
I take all of those outputs.

00:26:55.180 --> 00:26:57.790
and you have to remember that
there is a batch of outputs

00:26:57.790 --> 00:26:59.140
on each of those arrows.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.970
And I put them in the same bag.

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:04.630
Feed them through just
one softmax layer.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:09.140
And then, I will reshape them
back into the correct shape

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:11.410
to finish.

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:15.580
Again, using higher
level APIs in TensorFlow,

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:20.260
so when I call linear, that
just does the weighted sums.

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:23.580
One layer, it computes
simply the weighted sums.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.660
No activation function.

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:26.940
And then, I called softmax.

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:31.500
And that applies the
softmax activation function.

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:35.550
And linear, again, defines
the weights and biases

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:36.720
in the background.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:40.780
That's why I call it a
higher level function.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:43.470
And now, I'm ready to
compute my loss function

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:46.035
and derive it and actually
train the network.

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:54.590
It's just as complicated
to understand how

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:58.206
recurrent neural networks work.

00:27:58.206 --> 00:28:01.590
And it's just as
complicated to actually feed

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:04.240
them data correctly.

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:05.760
You see lots of arrows.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:09.450
So we will have to do
quite a bit of plumbing

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:11.560
to make this happen.

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:15.720
Let's try to get our inputs
and outputs right, OK?

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:18.600
So we will be inputting
sequences of characters

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:19.830
by batches.

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:24.240
So my inputs are a
batch of sequences.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:28.170
The sequence length that
I have chosen is 30.

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:31.120
I will be unrolling
over 30 characters.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:34.200
Usually, on the diagrams,
I only represent 8 of them

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:37.070
because 30 would
not fit on my slide.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:39.810
But in the code, it was 30.

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:44.430
Now, I need to
one-hot encode them.

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.230
So I'm adding a new size.

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:49.560
Each character becomes a
vector of 100 components

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:52.530
because I am working
with an alphabet of 100

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.212
possible characters.

00:28:54.212 --> 00:28:55.920
So now, it's batch
size, sequence length,

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:56.580
and alpha size.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:57.840
Those are my actual inputs.

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:04.360
My state, again, I
have a batch of states.

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.380
Since I'm feeding in
a batch of inputs,

00:29:06.380 --> 00:29:09.060
I will produce a batch
of output states.

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:12.070
And the states, each
of those state vectors

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:15.510
is, of course, of size
n, cell size, whatever

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:17.730
cell size I have chosen to use.

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:20.970
Remember, each cell has this
one configuration parameter,

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:23.520
which is its internal size.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:27.360
But since I have stacked
those cells three high,

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:31.290
it will actually-- the
actual output state here will

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.360
be three times the cell size.

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:40.690
OK, we are ready to
write this model.

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:44.280
So I define a placeholder
for my input sequences,

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:48.020
a batch of sequences of
size, sequence, length.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:51.210
I one-hot encode
them, which is why

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:54.840
I'm adding a new size
to this tensor, which

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.690
is the size of my alphabet.

00:29:56.690 --> 00:30:00.000
Again, each character becomes
a vector of 100 components.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:04.680
To be really precise, my alpha
size is 98, so 98 components.

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:08.310
I'm working with an alphabet
of 98 characters here.

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:12.306
I need to define a placeholder
for my correct answers.

00:30:12.306 --> 00:30:13.680
And actually, the
correct answers

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.390
are very easy to obtain here.

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:18.690
I'm just teaching it to output
the same sequence shifted

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:19.440
by one.

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:23.970
So basically, to predict what
the last character will be.

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:26.280
So again, the
correct answers will

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:29.280
be a batch of sequences
of 30 characters, which

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:31.440
I one-hot encode.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:35.520
I need a placeholder
also for my input state.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:38.630
And we have seen that the
batch of input states,

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:41.310
we have seen that the input
state is made of three

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:43.170
of those internal vectors.

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:47.510
So that's three times cell size.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:51.450
And now, I'm ready
to write my model.

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:54.390
So the model is
what was here, OK?

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:56.920
That's the model.

00:30:56.920 --> 00:31:03.900
This model, with this little
trick that we have seen before,

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:09.390
this model at the output
of its softmax layer

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:14.370
actually produces an output
that is batch size multiplied

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:15.270
by sequence length.

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:18.060
You remember, we put all the
characters from the batches

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.420
and from the different stages
of the unrolled sequence

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:22.580
in the same bag.

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:28.530
And now, to determine characters
from those probabilities,

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:30.180
I use argmax.

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:31.080
Why?

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:34.110
Because each of those
vectors is 100 components

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:35.720
with probabilities.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:40.740
Argmax is a function that gives
me the index of the biggest

00:31:40.740 --> 00:31:43.180
number in this vector.

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:45.240
So the index in this
victory is actually

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:50.460
the ASCII code of the character
that has been predicted.

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:53.520
So these are my
predictions now in ASCII

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:55.770
encoding in characters.

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:58.760
And I just need to reshape
them back to have, again,

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:03.290
a batch of sequences of
30 predicted characters.

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:10.140
And now, I'm ready to input--
to give my loss to an optimizer

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:16.440
and ask TensorFlow to optimize
to actually train my network.

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:20.060
So this is the step, as
yesterday, with this loss.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:21.800
TensorFlow computes a gradient.

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:24.300
From this gradient, it can--

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:24.800
sorry.

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:26.810
And this loss is, of
course, the difference

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:30.050
between the sequence of
characters that was predicted

00:32:30.050 --> 00:32:34.660
and the sequence of characters
that I wanted to predict.

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:37.430
This difference becomes a loss.

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:40.270
That loss is derived,
becomes a gradient.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.760
We take a small step
along this gradient,

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:45.070
which is actually in the
space of weights and biases.

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:48.700
So taking a small step means
we modify slightly our weights

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:51.370
and biases and continue.

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:54.040
That's the training.

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:56.980
One last little gotcha.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:00.610
So we have to take
our input text

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:05.170
and actually cut it up in those
sequences of 30 characters.

00:33:05.170 --> 00:33:07.789
So initially, I thought,
well, that's easy, you know?

00:33:07.789 --> 00:33:08.830
You take a piece of text.

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:10.829
How do you cut it up in
sequences of characters?

00:33:10.829 --> 00:33:12.880
Well, you cut, and
cut, and cut, and cut.

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:15.280
And then, if you
need a batch of them,

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:18.280
you take the first 100
sequences you have.

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:20.590
And you put that in a batch.

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:22.310
That did not work.

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:23.740
Why?

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:24.550
Let's see here.

00:33:24.550 --> 00:33:25.750
That's my first batch.

00:33:25.750 --> 00:33:27.800
Let's see the first
sequence in the batch?

00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:30.010
The quick-- you know
what that is going to be.

00:33:30.010 --> 00:33:32.380
The quick brown fox something.

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:37.480
Well, when my neural
network processes the quick,

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:41.230
it also outputs an output state.

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:44.890
And in the next iteration,
that output state

00:33:44.890 --> 00:33:48.820
will become the input state
for the next sequence.

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:52.420
If I want this to be
correct, that input state

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:54.880
must correspond to
the continuation

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:57.970
of the quick brown
fox, and so on,

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:00.550
which means that
the sentence has

00:34:00.550 --> 00:34:04.330
to continue over all of
the first slots of all

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:06.270
of my batches.

00:34:06.270 --> 00:34:12.070
It's a not completely
trivial way of batching here.

00:34:12.070 --> 00:34:14.980
You cut up your text in
batches, in sequences.

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:16.690
But the way to
batch them together,

00:34:16.690 --> 00:34:20.440
since you have to pass the
correct state at each stage,

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:22.750
is that the
beginning of the text

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:26.469
has to be split across
the first item in batches.

00:34:26.469 --> 00:34:30.699
And then, from some point far,
far, far later in the text,

00:34:30.699 --> 00:34:34.060
you can start filling the
second line of the batches.

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:39.310
It's just plumbing.

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:46.120
I wrote for you the five
lines of code that does this.

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:46.870
It's five lines.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:51.130
I spent four hours doing
it, including tests.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:53.020
I don't do arithmetic.

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:55.659
It's full of
modulos and divides.

00:34:55.659 --> 00:34:58.990
And I wrote unit
tests and hacked it

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:02.374
until the unit tests passed.

00:35:02.374 --> 00:35:03.790
It's called
test-driven debugging.

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:07.240
Sorry, test-driven development.

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:09.130
That's what developers do.

00:35:09.130 --> 00:35:11.480
All right, so yeah, small
gotcha on the batching.

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:12.310
But whatever.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:13.840
Just use the code on--

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:15.310
this is not actually important.

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:21.471
Just use the function that
will cut up the text correctly

00:35:21.471 --> 00:35:21.970
for you.

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:23.053
And you're ready to train.

00:35:23.053 --> 00:35:25.720
And this is actually the full
code of this neural network

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.460
on one slide.

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:30.160
So let's go through this again.

00:35:30.160 --> 00:35:32.940
A placeholder for
my input sequences.

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:34.150
I one-hot encode them.

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:37.810
I'm actually inputting
sequences of characters, OK?

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:41.770
And all the people with
cameras, this is on GitHub.

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:44.390
And the GitHub link
is on the last slide.

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:47.110
So please take pictures.

00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:49.780
My Twitter handle is over there.

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:51.010
Tweet them.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:53.500
But then, you will be able
to go and GitHub and actually

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:55.480
retrieve this.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:36:00.090
Then, my expected
outputs, why underscore?

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.320
Again, I define a
placeholder for them.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.930
I will need to feed
them during training.

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:08.560
And the first thing I do is
that I one-hot encode them.

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:10.690
I will also need,
and this is different

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:12.670
from normal neural
networks, I will also

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:15.490
need a placeholder
for my input state.

00:36:15.490 --> 00:36:16.110
Remember?

00:36:16.110 --> 00:36:20.170
RNNs have an input
and an input state.

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:21.760
Two inputs.

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:23.800
Now, I'm ready to
write my model.

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:25.510
So I chose the GRU cell.

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:27.130
I stack it three high.

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:33.100
And I unroll it as many times
as x has components in it.

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:37.300
So here, my unroll size
is sequence length.

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:38.230
And that's 30.

00:36:38.230 --> 00:36:41.980
I chose 30 characters
as the unroll size

00:36:41.980 --> 00:36:45.400
of my recurring neural network.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:47.500
I do my little trick
with the softmax

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:51.610
so that I can implement
just one softmax node.

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:55.090
I feed the output
through my softmax node.

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:59.590
Here, I apply argmax to retrieve
from the softmax probabilities

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:00.800
the highest probability.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.040
And that's the character
I'm predicting.

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:09.040
I reshape this back to have a
batch of predicted sequences.

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:11.030
Also, somewhere in
the middle in there,

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:12.880
I had those probabilities.

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:14.440
I take those probabilities.

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:18.400
And I compute the distance
between what it says

00:37:18.400 --> 00:37:19.420
and what I wanted.

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:20.560
That's my loss.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:22.540
I give my loss to the optimizer.

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:23.890
I obtain a training step.

00:37:23.890 --> 00:37:25.930
And this training
step is actually

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:30.145
that gradient, which is computed
on this batch of training

00:37:30.145 --> 00:37:30.880
characters.

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:33.100
And which, if I follow
it by a little step,

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:35.050
will modify my
weights and biases

00:37:35.050 --> 00:37:39.310
and bring me to somewhere where
this network works better,

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:42.250
where it has a smaller
error function.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:44.170
And now, my training loop.

00:37:44.170 --> 00:37:46.390
You will see, this is
very familiar to what

00:37:46.390 --> 00:37:48.670
we had previously.

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:50.920
We use this magic
plumbing function

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:54.670
that I gave you to load
sequences of characters

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.740
in the correct way.

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:59.380
And once I have a
sequence of characters,

00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:03.850
I run session.run
of my training step.

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:06.000
I have to give it
the input characters.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.590
I have to give it
the expected output.

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:11.830
And, since this is a
recurrent neural network,

00:38:11.830 --> 00:38:15.750
I have to give it
the input state.

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.380
And this will give
me an output state.

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:20.140
And you see the
magic line, why this

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:21.820
is a recurrent neural network.

00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:25.030
That's the last line
there in the red.

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:28.420
Input state becomes--
sorry, the output state

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:30.417
becomes an input state.

00:38:30.417 --> 00:38:31.500
That's why it's recurrent.

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:34.090
They're passing
the state around.

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:36.100
All right.

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:36.910
So we are done.

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:39.070
We've built a recurrent
neural network.

00:38:39.070 --> 00:38:41.920
Now, we want to
actually train it.

00:38:41.920 --> 00:38:44.920
So let's go to a demo.

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:53.220
I will be training this on
the complete works of William

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:55.560
Shakespeare.

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:57.420
That's not quite big data.

00:38:57.420 --> 00:39:01.440
The complete works of William
Shakespeare are five megabytes.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:04.187
Yes, that puts things
in perspective.

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:08.520
But it's good stuff.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:16.580
So here, we see it's
training on sequences.

00:39:16.580 --> 00:39:19.350
So here are those
sequences of 30 characters.

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:21.210
And here's a batch of them.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:23.130
It's actually training
on those sequences.

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:25.500
Here, predicting
not much at all.

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:27.330
It's just the beginning.

00:39:27.330 --> 00:39:32.100
And from time to time,
I stop the training.

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:34.110
And I take just my one cell.

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:35.850
Remember, I have just one cell.

00:39:35.850 --> 00:39:38.580
It's replicated for the
purpose of training,

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:39.885
but it's just one cell.

00:39:39.885 --> 00:39:44.090
And this one cell,
it has become--

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:46.870
well, once it will be
trained, it will have become--

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:48.970
a language model.

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:52.511
So what I can do with it is
generate a new Shakespeare

00:39:52.511 --> 00:39:53.010
play.

00:39:55.730 --> 00:39:57.240
How do I do that?

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:59.090
Well, I take the cell.

00:39:59.090 --> 00:40:01.580
I put in garbage,
a random character.

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:05.090
That gives me an
output character.

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:07.010
Probability of an
output character,

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:09.887
which is the next character,
and an output state.

00:40:09.887 --> 00:40:11.720
You feed back the output
state in the input,

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:14.300
and I feed back the output
character as the new input.

00:40:14.300 --> 00:40:15.710
And I continue.

00:40:15.710 --> 00:40:20.270
And this is a state machine
that will start generating text.

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:22.880
You see here, it's--

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:24.830
yeah.

00:40:24.830 --> 00:40:28.730
That's not quite
Shakespeare yet.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:30.150
It's training.

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:31.490
It's a bit slow on my machine.

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:33.600
I usually have a
GPU connected here

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:39.140
to-- it brings me a nice
10x speed, or 6x roughly.

00:40:39.140 --> 00:40:42.590
But still, well, it has
done 50 more batches.

00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:44.090
I will leave it running.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:48.777
Let's go and see, sorry, here.

00:40:48.777 --> 00:40:49.860
On this slide, what it is.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:52.520
So at the beginning,
it gives you this.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:54.410
As I said, not
quite Shakespeare.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:59.300
But after only an epoch,
what we call an epoch,

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:04.030
it's when you have seen the
entire training data set once.

00:41:04.030 --> 00:41:06.230
So after having
seen only a tenth

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:10.720
of what Shakespeare produced in
his life, this is what we have.

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.800
Still not quite
Shakespeare, but you see

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:15.070
there is some structure to it.

00:41:15.070 --> 00:41:19.630
After two tenths, hey,
this looks better!

00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:24.680
It's starting to actually
spell English almost correctly.

00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:27.680
And there are those
things in capital letters

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:29.150
at the beginning
that are starting

00:41:29.150 --> 00:41:32.090
to look like characters,
like character names.

00:41:32.090 --> 00:41:37.720
Even slightly later, oh, look!

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:40.700
And you have to
remember that this

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:44.180
is a neural network that
is predicting character

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:45.170
by character.

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:50.030
It first has to learn to
spell English before going

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:53.490
to higher orders of structure.

00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:56.240
So it's still not
completely exact English.

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:59.240
But it's starting to
look like English.

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:01.190
At least, Shakespearean English.

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:03.190
And you see it has
character names.

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:06.080
And it's actually inventing
new character names.

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:08.810
Here, Pordia and Henry Blutius--

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:12.080
who can tell me,
no, seriously, who

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:14.120
can tell me if
Shakespeare actually

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:17.970
used Henry Blutius in his work?

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:19.220
What do you call it?

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:20.470
I'm giving you the answer.

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:21.350
He didn't.

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:24.280
But it's a very credible
Shakespearean character name.

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:29.610
And this is what you
get after 30 epochs.

00:42:29.610 --> 00:42:31.294
So it actually has a title.

00:42:31.294 --> 00:42:31.960
There is an act.

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:33.070
There's a scene.

00:42:33.070 --> 00:42:36.860
After the scene, it tells
you where this is happening.

00:42:36.860 --> 00:42:40.700
And look, it knows how
to put scenic indications

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:45.110
in brackets, who enters,
with whom, and so on.

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:49.040
It has even picked up stuff
like, character names are

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:52.640
all caps, and when the character
is a function, like Lord

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:55.160
or Chamberlain, it's
only the first character

00:42:55.160 --> 00:42:56.330
that is a capital.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:58.610
It has picked up completely
correctly as well.

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:03.620
And it's actually English.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:07.730
So now that we have
this, let's try to--

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:08.930
so this was on slides.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:10.520
I will stop this.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:12.890
What I have done previously
is that I trained

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:15.500
this for actually 30 epochs.

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:18.500
And I saved my
weights and biases.

00:43:18.500 --> 00:43:23.450
So I'm ready to just replay it
and generate a new Shakespeare

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:24.650
play.

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:28.430
Let's generate a new one
live in front of you.

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:29.336
Here it is.

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:34.090
Let me stop it.

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:36.264
Whoops, sorry about that.

00:43:36.264 --> 00:43:38.172
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:42.647 --> 00:43:44.230
MARTIN GORNER: Is
someone brave enough

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:47.830
to come and play hallucinated
Shakespeare on the stage

00:43:47.830 --> 00:43:48.459
with me?

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:54.478
Come on.

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:00.020
Yes!

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:01.370
Thank you.

00:44:01.370 --> 00:44:02.636
Big applause.

00:44:02.636 --> 00:44:03.470
SPEAKER: Come on up?

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:04.511
MARTIN GORNER: Thank you.

00:44:04.511 --> 00:44:05.491
Please, come up.

00:44:05.491 --> 00:44:06.740
You will have to speak loudly.

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:08.180
But that's how it
is in a theater.

00:44:08.180 --> 00:44:09.346
You don't have a microphone.

00:44:09.346 --> 00:44:10.950
You speak.

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:13.280
So you can read off
the screen here.

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:14.920
We will alternate.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:18.770
So maybe I start, and
then you do the next one.

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:21.500
So let's say enter
Bardolph and Boult.

00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:25.160
The manner off with my bestowers
that you shall not see him,

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:28.310
and we are now to be the
brother's wife and force,

00:44:28.310 --> 00:44:32.100
to be so many and most grave.

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:34.590
SPEAKER: What art thou again?

00:44:34.590 --> 00:44:36.600
What needs thy life?

00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:40.050
Then, what they do
not dote on thee.

00:44:43.062 --> 00:44:46.450
The word will be at thee.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:48.420
And take my heart to thee.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:50.520
And they distemper.

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:55.825
Will thou beat me well
to say god save my son?

00:44:55.825 --> 00:44:57.805
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:59.225 --> 00:45:00.600
MARTIN GORNER:
Thank you so much.

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:01.180
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:02.221
MARTIN GORNER: Thank you.

00:45:04.230 --> 00:45:05.290
That was fantastic.

00:45:05.290 --> 00:45:07.410
Thank you.

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:12.030
Actually, I tried to do
this also on the Python code

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:14.220
of TensorFlow itself.

00:45:14.220 --> 00:45:14.850
That was fun.

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:22.440
So in the beginning,
you had this.

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:24.570
Looks like Python?

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:25.940
Maybe.

00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:27.930
But very, very
quickly, it actually

00:45:27.930 --> 00:45:32.040
picks up Pythonic structures,
like those keywords,

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:36.850
and it's generating something
that looks like function calls.

00:45:36.850 --> 00:45:43.190
Slightly later, it actually
correctly uses the keywords

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:45.920
with function names-- a
hallucinated function name.

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:50.180
It's actually quite inventive
in the function names.

00:45:50.180 --> 00:45:51.770
And a colon at the end.

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:56.330
It's still getting the
nested parenthesis wrong.

00:45:56.330 --> 00:46:00.470
And after a longer while, it
can recite the Apache license

00:46:00.470 --> 00:46:01.010
in full.

00:46:07.150 --> 00:46:08.350
Yes.

00:46:08.350 --> 00:46:12.910
It's open source compliant,
open source compliant.

00:46:12.910 --> 00:46:15.850
And more interestingly
for us, designers

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:18.760
of recurrent neural networks,
it can actually close and open

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:22.030
the nested parentheses
right to a depth of three,

00:46:22.030 --> 00:46:24.250
which is quite impressive.

00:46:24.250 --> 00:46:28.660
And what I find fantastic,
it's that it has figured out

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:32.290
how to do Python comments.

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:34.840
And it's giving me TensorFlow
advice in those comments.

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:41.140
But look, it makes sense!

00:46:41.140 --> 00:46:42.810
Check that we have
both scalar tensors

00:46:42.810 --> 00:46:44.379
for being invalid
to a vector of one

00:46:44.379 --> 00:46:46.170
indicating the total
loss of the same shape

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:49.440
as the shape of the tensor.

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:51.630
I'm sure this makes just
as much sense as everything

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:53.588
that I've been saying
since the beginning here.

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:00.800
All right, and small
credits to a gentleman

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:04.370
called Andrej
Karpathy who actually

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:06.942
wrote this neural network
for the first time.

00:47:06.942 --> 00:47:08.150
He published a blog about it.

00:47:08.150 --> 00:47:10.550
He tried it on many
different things.

00:47:10.550 --> 00:47:13.145
He generated a business
book for startups.

00:47:17.560 --> 00:47:23.600
And he tried to generate
an algebra book in LaTeX.

00:47:23.600 --> 00:47:27.650
Actually, after training, this
produced almost valid LaTeX.

00:47:27.650 --> 00:47:30.650
So he had to hack it a little
bit to make it compile.

00:47:30.650 --> 00:47:32.450
But then, this looks
like an algebra book.

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:36.530
That is even an
attempt at a diagram.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:40.730
And the line I prefer is
how the neural network

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:44.480
solved how to write a proof.

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:46.070
Look at the very top.

00:47:46.070 --> 00:47:46.900
"Proof omitted."

00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:52.180
That's so clever.

00:47:56.250 --> 00:47:58.010
All right.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:00.456
So that's basically all
I wanted to show you.

00:48:00.456 --> 00:48:01.830
Well, this is how
we generate it.

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:04.430
So we, I take just one cell.

00:48:04.430 --> 00:48:06.650
And basically, in a loop,
I feed in a character.

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:08.540
I take the output, feed
it back as the input,

00:48:08.540 --> 00:48:12.440
and feed the output
state as the input state,

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:14.750
and just do this in a loop.

00:48:14.750 --> 00:48:16.850
A couple of
applications of this.

00:48:16.850 --> 00:48:18.080
Oh, yes.

00:48:18.080 --> 00:48:20.570
Actually, we still have
a little bit of time.

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:26.180
This time, I've been using
TensorBoard to visualize

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:28.430
my inputs and outputs.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:30.730
Where is my TensorBoard?

00:48:30.730 --> 00:48:31.290
Somewhere.

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:32.170
Sorry, I'll find it.

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:34.050
Here.

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:36.480
In the last session, I was
just throwing the outputs

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:44.430
into matplotlib, which is the
very standard Python plotting

00:48:44.430 --> 00:48:45.450
library.

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:49.020
But there is a tool dedicated
to visualizing training

00:48:49.020 --> 00:48:49.780
in TensorFlow.

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:52.610
It's called TensorBoard.

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.780
And I advise you to
use it, especially

00:48:54.780 --> 00:48:56.700
if you do a distributed
training, or training

00:48:56.700 --> 00:48:57.960
on remote servers.

00:48:57.960 --> 00:49:00.690
It can connect to a bucket and
get the information from there

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:02.950
and visualize it.

00:49:02.950 --> 00:49:04.930
So here again, I
have configured,

00:49:04.930 --> 00:49:06.580
when I was training
this network,

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:10.210
I configured it to actually
do training and validation.

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:13.790
I put one Shakespeare
play aside for validation

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:15.500
to test my network.

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:19.240
And if you'll remember the
session from yesterday,

00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:24.940
I find it very important to
follow my loss curves, both

00:49:24.940 --> 00:49:30.000
the training and the test
loss curve, on the screen.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.240
This is what I got.

00:49:32.240 --> 00:49:37.910
And actually, first of all,
who sees something wrong?

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:42.960
Overfit, yeah.

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:48.510
And so now, the question is,
why is it overfitting here?

00:49:48.510 --> 00:49:51.480
I will give you the answer
because you can't guess it.

00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:55.560
But here, I was actually
training on a small subset

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:58.470
of the Shakespeare corpus.

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:04.430
So here, it was overfitting
because of lack of data.

00:50:04.430 --> 00:50:07.250
And since I had
this on the curves,

00:50:07.250 --> 00:50:12.640
I wanted to show it to you
because you certainly remember

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:17.443
that somewhere, where is it?

00:50:17.443 --> 00:50:19.330
This one.

00:50:19.330 --> 00:50:23.930
Somewhere here, I had this
helpful engineering chart.

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:30.450
Which allows you to interpret
what overfitting is.

00:50:30.450 --> 00:50:33.090
And we went yesterday
through the bad network.

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:34.920
We went through
too many neurons.

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:36.330
We never had not enough data.

00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:38.100
So I tried with not enough data.

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:42.630
And yes, it also gives you
this very recognizable pattern

00:50:42.630 --> 00:50:43.660
in the curves.

00:50:43.660 --> 00:50:48.040
And as soon as I train with
more data, this is what I have.

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:51.460
So here, the two curves
follow each other closely.

00:50:51.460 --> 00:50:53.770
And I know that I have
solved the problem.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:55.570
So actually, I was
doing this because I

00:50:55.570 --> 00:50:59.440
was trying to add
dropout into my network

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:00.940
to make it work better.

00:51:00.940 --> 00:51:01.600
No.

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:04.140
It was misbehaving just
because of lack of data.

00:51:04.140 --> 00:51:07.520
Dropout would not
have solved that.

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:12.050
All right, and so a
couple of applications,

00:51:12.050 --> 00:51:14.890
practical applications
to finish.

00:51:14.890 --> 00:51:19.570
We've seen how to produce a
character by character model.

00:51:25.120 --> 00:51:27.780
We can also use this not
character by character,

00:51:27.780 --> 00:51:29.430
but word by word.

00:51:29.430 --> 00:51:31.350
So as I said
previously, with a word,

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:33.900
it's a bit more complicated
because to one-hot encode

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:37.800
a word, you need to encode
it on a vector of, this time,

00:51:37.800 --> 00:51:39.210
30,000 components.

00:51:39.210 --> 00:51:41.610
Because that's the typical
size of a vocabulary,

00:51:41.610 --> 00:51:43.240
typical language.

00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:44.730
So those are big.

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:46.680
So on the inputs,
there is actually

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:48.000
a very simple solution.

00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:50.430
How do you reduce the
size of a big vector?

00:51:50.430 --> 00:51:52.560
Well, you use one layer
of a neural network

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:56.100
and produce less outputs.

00:51:56.100 --> 00:51:59.070
It's called embeddings.

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:03.490
And that layer can either
be part of your training--

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:05.710
then, your embeddings
are learned

00:52:05.710 --> 00:52:07.450
as part of the training.

00:52:07.450 --> 00:52:09.970
Or, you can use
some neural network

00:52:09.970 --> 00:52:13.420
that has been already
trained, typically trained

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:16.420
on the English
language generically,

00:52:16.420 --> 00:52:19.740
and that just encodes
words into smaller vectors.

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:24.790
There is a very
famous, what is it,

00:52:24.790 --> 00:52:26.740
neural network that has
been built for that.

00:52:26.740 --> 00:52:28.330
It's called Word2Vec.

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:31.300
Already trained,
available on GitHub.

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:34.210
You can use that to
encode your English words

00:52:34.210 --> 00:52:36.160
if your problem deals
with English words

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:38.200
as smaller vectors.

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:40.690
And so once we have
solved this problem of how

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:43.120
to input words
instead of characters,

00:52:43.120 --> 00:52:45.520
you can, for example, use
a recurrent neural network

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:49.120
like this to predict not
what the next word is,

00:52:49.120 --> 00:52:53.570
but a categorization
of a sequence.

00:52:53.570 --> 00:52:56.410
And this is used in newspapers
to automatically categorize

00:52:56.410 --> 00:53:00.154
articles as geopolitics,
science, sports, and so on.

00:53:00.154 --> 00:53:00.820
Works very well.

00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:06.880
How do you do translation?

00:53:06.880 --> 00:53:10.230
Well, to do translation, that's
how Google Translate works,

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:14.130
you tack two of those
recurrent networks end to end.

00:53:14.130 --> 00:53:17.550
To the first one, you
apply an English sentence

00:53:17.550 --> 00:53:19.890
plus a stop symbol.

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:23.320
And then, you continue.

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:27.610
And you ask it to output
the French sentence.

00:53:27.610 --> 00:53:30.490
And what you have on the
input, there is a choice.

00:53:30.490 --> 00:53:32.980
Normally, you
should be inputting

00:53:32.980 --> 00:53:35.320
what your network outputs.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:39.400
But people have
also tried to input

00:53:39.400 --> 00:53:41.710
what the network should output.

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:43.840
So both options exist.

00:53:43.840 --> 00:53:46.400
And they give you
different results.

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:48.850
You can read about
this in literature.

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:52.330
So this is how
translation works.

00:53:52.330 --> 00:53:54.550
Of course, you have a
big problem at the end.

00:53:54.550 --> 00:53:56.410
I won't go into that.

00:53:56.410 --> 00:53:59.260
Because to do the
softmax layers there,

00:53:59.260 --> 00:54:04.510
you actually want to produce a
vector of 30,000 probabilities.

00:54:04.510 --> 00:54:06.110
That's a bit heavy.

00:54:06.110 --> 00:54:08.710
So there are ways
of mitigating that.

00:54:08.710 --> 00:54:10.900
But that's an active
area of research.

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:13.690
One that is implemented
in TensorFlow

00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:16.457
is called sample softmax.

00:54:16.457 --> 00:54:18.040
But there are many
others because this

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:19.290
is an active area of research.

00:54:19.290 --> 00:54:23.080
How to do this softmax layer
to produce 30,000 probabilities

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:26.040
each time, which is a bit heavy.

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:29.110
And one more is image labeling.

00:54:29.110 --> 00:54:32.140
So here, it's a very simplified
version of image labeling.

00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:33.772
Image labeling is
you take an image,

00:54:33.772 --> 00:54:35.230
and you want to
produce a sentence.

00:54:35.230 --> 00:54:38.170
Like, this a little girl
holding a teddy bear.

00:54:38.170 --> 00:54:40.300
This is a truck in the desert.

00:54:40.300 --> 00:54:44.170
So this is actually also
a translation problem.

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:47.470
You take vectors from an image.

00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:50.710
And you apply a
recurrent neural network

00:54:50.710 --> 00:54:53.380
to produce a sequence
of words which

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:58.150
you want to be the
description of this image.

00:54:58.150 --> 00:55:00.150
How do you encode an
image as a vector?

00:55:00.150 --> 00:55:02.450
Well, there are
plenty of solutions.

00:55:02.450 --> 00:55:04.660
One of them is to
take an off the shelf

00:55:04.660 --> 00:55:07.540
image recognition neural
network, like Inception,

00:55:07.540 --> 00:55:10.930
and just chop off the
last couple of layers.

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:13.420
Normally, what Inception
gives is categories.

00:55:13.420 --> 00:55:14.350
This is a truck.

00:55:14.350 --> 00:55:15.160
This is a beach.

00:55:15.160 --> 00:55:16.900
This is a lizard.

00:55:16.900 --> 00:55:18.570
That's not what you want.

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:20.860
But all the top
layers are actually

00:55:20.860 --> 00:55:25.450
encoding an image in some
meaningful way into a vector.

00:55:25.450 --> 00:55:28.090
You can use that as a
fixed encoding function.

00:55:28.090 --> 00:55:30.880
And input the vector
corresponding to the image

00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:31.720
here.

00:55:31.720 --> 00:55:34.660
Produce this output sequence.

00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:37.550
And sometimes, it
works really well.

00:55:37.550 --> 00:55:38.770
This is what was generated.

00:55:38.770 --> 00:55:41.410
A herd of elephants walking
across a dry grass field,

00:55:41.410 --> 00:55:43.450
and so on.

00:55:43.450 --> 00:55:48.520
And then sometimes,
yeah, not quite.

00:55:52.730 --> 00:55:54.470
Thank you.

00:55:54.470 --> 00:55:55.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:55.970 --> 00:55:58.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

